# NYC early voting site distance disparate impact tests, by tract and election district subdivision

## Executive summary

**Question.** Are higher poverty parts of NYC more likely to be assigned, by election district, to an early voting site more than a quarter mile away? The unit is a subdivision, the overlap of one tract with one election district, which keeps its tract's poverty rate. ({{ r.tracts_analyzed }} subdivisions from {{ x.tracts }} tracts, {{ r.poll_sites }} early voting sites.)

**Answer.** {% if s.supported %}The baseline hypothesis is supported: higher poverty subdivisions are more likely to be assigned to a site more than a quarter mile away.{% else %}The baseline hypothesis is not supported: higher poverty subdivisions are not more likely to be assigned to a site more than a quarter mile away.{% if s.opposite_and_significant %} The association runs the opposite way.{% endif %}{% endif %}


- Across the five poverty classes, {{ s.first.rate | pct }} of {{ s.first.group | lower }} subdivisions are assigned to a site over a quarter mile away, against {{ s.last.rate | pct }} of {{ s.last.group | lower }} subdivisions. Chi-squared = {{ "%.2f" | format(c.chi2) }}, p = {{ c.p | p }}, Cramér's V = {{ "%.3f" | format(c.cramers_v) }}, trend test one-sided p = {{ b.trend.p_one_sided | p }}.
- Disparate impact: subdivisions at {{ "%.0f" | format(HIGH_POVERTY) }}% poverty or above are assigned to a site within a quarter mile {{ di.favorable_rate_high | pct }} of the time, those below that {{ di.favorable_rate_lower | pct }}. The impact ratio is {{ "%.2f" | format(di.impact_ratio) }}, which {{ "fails" if di.fails_four_fifths else "passes" }} the four-fifths rule.
- Most subdivisions of every kind are more than a quarter mile from their assigned site. The overall rate is {{ s.overall_out_rate | pct }}.
- Related tests that are significant after Holm correction and contradict the hypothesis: {{ s.opposite | join(", ") or "none" }}. Significant and consistent with it: {{ s.toward | join(", ") or "none" }}.
- Distance (H3 and H4, centroid to the assigned site at a half and one mile): higher poverty subdivisions are assigned to sites {{ "farther" if s.distance_farther else "closer" }} than lower poverty subdivisions. H5 repeats the test for an assigned site in a different tract.

**Caution.** Neighboring subdivisions are not independent, and subdivisions of one tract share a poverty rate, so p-values are probably too small. Subdivisions are counted equally whatever their size or population. See Limits.

Subdivisions analyzed: {{ r.tracts_analyzed }} with a poverty rate ({{ r.tracts_with_income }} also with median income), from {{ x.tracts }} tracts. {{ x.split_tracts }} tracts span more than one election district and are split; the rest are one subdivision. {{ x.tracts_without_district }} tracts overlap no election district. Overlaps narrower than 10 meters, where tract and district boundaries disagree, are dropped: {{ x.slivers_dropped }} slivers, {{ x.sliver_area_share | pct }} of the overlap area. {{ r.poll_sites_outside_tracts }} of the {{ r.poll_sites }} assigned sites lie outside every analyzed tract.

